    "body": "AdventHealth took the No. 1 spot in Gartner ’s Healthcare Supply Chain Top 25 for the third straight year. The health system earned a composite score of 8.06 in the 2026 ranking, ahead of Stanford Medicine at 7.45 and Bon Secours Mercy Health at 7.33.\nWhat’s Related\nThe top four held their places from last year, with Corewell Health again at No. 4. Northwestern Medicine climbed four spots to No. 5, while Kaiser Permanente debuted at No. 6. Houston Methodist also made its first appearance at No. 24.\nElsewhere on the list, Mass General Brigham rose from No. 25 to No. 14, and Johns Hopkins Medicine moved from No. 21 to No. 15. Gartner also recognized Mayo Clinic, Intermountain Health, and Cleveland Clinic in its Masters category, which requires a top-five composite score in at least seven of the past 10 years. Mayo Clinic and Intermountain Health earned that designation for the ninth year in a row, and Cleveland Clinic earned it for the third time.\nRELATED: Top 25 Healthcare Supply Chain Rankings for 2025\nBehind the rankings\nGartner’s annual ranking, now in its 18th year, evaluates U.S. health systems. Gartner analysts’ assessments and peer opinions each account for 25% of the composite score. Patient care quality makes up 20%, bond ratings 15%, environmental, social, and governance measures 10%, and risk management 5%.\nThe published scores are rounded to two decimal places, and Gartner uses the underlying scores to set the order. That’s why NewYork-Presbyterian ranks No. 21, and UPMC ranks No. 22 even though both show a score of 4.66.\nWhat the top-ranked systems are doing\nGartner found that leading health systems are giving doctors and other clinical staff a bigger say in which products they buy and use. Some are appointing medical directors to their supply chain teams. Others are linking procedure supply lists to electronic health records to cut waste and reduce variation in what clinicians use.\nHealth systems are also setting up inventory hubs and looking further down their supply chains for signs of disruption . Gartner found that some are pairing AI tools that flag supply risks with lists of substitutes clinicians have already approved. Health systems are also using AI in areas such as contract management and disruption monitoring.\nThe full 2026 ranking\nRank | Health system | Composite score\n1 | AdventHealth | 8.06\n2 | Stanford Medicine | 7.45\n3 | Bon Secours Mercy Health | 7.33\n4 | Corewell Health | 6.79\n5 | Northwestern Medicine | 6.63\n6 | Kaiser Permanente | 6.62\n7 | Rush University System for Health | 6.39\n8 | BJC Health | 6.35\n9 | Advocate Health | 5.79\n10 | Sutter Health | 5.58\n11 | St. Luke’s Health System | 5.53\n12 | Allina Health | 5.50\n13 | Indiana University Health | 5.44\n14 | Mass General Brigham | 5.26\n15 | Johns Hopkins Medicine | 5.24\n16 | Vanderbilt Health | 5.18\n17 | Endeavor Health | 5.15\n18 | CommonSpirit Health | 4.89\n19 | Duke Health | 4.73\n20 | Inova Health System | 4.72\n21 | NewYork-Presbyterian | 4.66\n22 | UPMC | 4.66\n23 | Baylor Scott & White Health | 4.54\n24 | Houston Methodist | 4.52\n25 | Sentara Health | 4.35",
    "body_sha256": "05573a36cfb272bed2820e40353c26c50a33a0a39aa9a4fd95b1576ddb5a7442",
